King of Connacht and later High King of Ireland in 1156."}, {"sourceSlug": "anglo-norman-invasion-of-ireland", "sourceName": "Anglo-Norman Invasion of Ireland", "verb": "OCCURS_DURING", "targetSlug": "tairrdelbach-ua-conchobair", "targetName": "Tairrdelbach Ua Conchobair", "context": "The period following Tairrdelbach's death in 1156 saw the Anglo-Norman Invasion of Ireland begin in 1169, transforming the political landscape he had shaped."}, {"sourceSlug": "synod-of-kells-1152", "sourceName": "Synod of Kells (1152)", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "tairrdelbach-ua-conchobair", "targetName": "Tairrdelbach Ua Conchobair", "context": "The ecclesiastical reforms of the Synod of Kells in 1152, which restructured the Irish church, occurred during Tairrdelbach's reign and influenced the political landscape he navigated."}], "places": [{"name": "Connacht, Ireland", "role": "Kingdom's Base"}, {"name": "Athlone, Ireland", "role": "Strategic Crossing Point"}, {"name": "Lough Corrib, Ireland", "role": "Naval Power Projection"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Tairrdelbach Ua Conchobair", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "tairrdelbach-ua-conchobair"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q2740972", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tairrdelbach_Ua_Conchobair"], "tags": ["Ireland", "Monarchs & Rulers", "Europe", "political-systems", "empire-and-colonialism", "geopolitical-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Tairrdelbach Ua Conchobair significantly shaped 12th-century Ireland by unifying much of the island under Connacht's dominance, effectively creating the most powerful native high kingship before the Anglo-Norman invasion. His efforts set the stage for subsequent attempts at Irish centralization, even as they ultimately failed to prevent foreign conquest.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-09-09T00:56:51Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#34296085407", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Toirdhealbhach Mór Ua Conchobhair anglicised Turlough Mór O'Conor, was King of Connacht (1106–1156) and High King of Ireland.", "newValue": "Tairrdelbach Ua Conchobair, known as Toirdhealbhach Mór Ua Conchobhair, was the King of Connacht from 1106 until his death in 1156 and High King of Ireland, a pivotal figure who sought to consolidate a truly unified high kingship. Often regarded as the most powerful native Irish monarch prior to the Anglo-Norman invasion, his reign marked a zenith of Gaelic Irish political and military strength in the 12th century, preceding the significant foreign incursions that would transform the island's political landscape.

Tairrdelbach's rule was defined by relentless military expansion, strategic political maneuvering, and significant infrastructure projects. He successfully asserted dominance over much of Ireland, compelling submission from other provincial kings, including those of Munster, Leinster, and Ulster through a series of campaigns and alliances. Notably, he oversaw the construction of crucial bridges across the Shannon River and established an impressive fleet on Lough Corrib, which he employed to project power and control vital trade routes, thereby expanding Connacht's influence further than any predecessor.

The structures of Tairrdelbach's high kingship, while formidable, proved ultimately impermanent, heavily reliant on his personal military genius and political acumen rather than institutionalized statecraft. Though he did not establish a lasting, centralized state comparable to contemporary European monarchies, his efforts laid the groundwork for a more integrated, albeit fragile, Irish political landscape that his son, Ruaidrí Ua Conchobair, would inherit. Ruaidrí briefly succeeded him as High King, attempting to further centralize power just prior to the arrival of the Normans, a cataclysmic event that would irrevocably alter Ireland's future."}, {"timestamp": "2026-09-09T00:56:51Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#34296085407", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1088, Connacht"}, {"title": "Dynastic succession and sovereign authority traditions", "type": "Idea", "year": "1088, Connacht"}], "newValue": ["The ongoing fragmentation of the older Uí Néill high kingship created a power vacuum that ambitious provincial rulers like Tairrdelbach could exploit.", "Connacht's strategic geographical position and growing internal strength under the Ua Conchobair dynasty enabled its significant expansionist policies.", "The 12th-century ecclesiastical reform movement in Ireland, culminating in events like the Synod of Kells (1152), indirectly created a more unified legal and religious framework that Tairrdelbach sought to align with his political ambitions."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-09-09T00:56:51Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#34296085407", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Established dynastic legacies and territorial boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "1156"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "